      "S3",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Jack Nicklaus, known as the Golden Bear, is an American golfer who won a record eighteen professional major championships. He built a second career designing golf courses and developing businesses around his name, from branded products to international licensing. His family participates in the enterprise he established alongside his playing career.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Working behind his father's pharmacy counter gave Jack Nicklaus an early introduction to business. The future golfer studied pre-pharmacy at Ohio State and sold insurance before turning professional at twenty-one. His father Charlie, who introduced him to golf as a child, remained a close mentor.\n\nNicklaus and his wife Barbara founded the Nicklaus Children's Health Care Foundation in 2004. It joined other donors and healthcare providers to expand paediatric services at Jupiter Medical Center in Florida. Their partnership helped open a dedicated children's emergency department, with child-sized medical equipment and family waiting areas designed to make hospital visits less unsettling.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Official life history and foundation project account support complementary formative and philanthropic dimensions."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S6"
    ],
    "word_count": 100,
    "html": "<p>Working behind his father's pharmacy counter gave Jack Nicklaus an early introduction to business. The future golfer studied pre-pharmacy at Ohio State and sold insurance before turning professional at twenty-one. His father Charlie, who introduced him to golf as a child, remained a close mentor.</p>\r\n<p>Nicklaus and his wife Barbara founded the Nicklaus Children's Health Care Foundation in 2004. It joined other donors and healthcare providers to expand paediatric services at Jupiter Medical Center in Florida. Their partnership helped open a dedicated children's emergency department, with child-sized medical equipment and family waiting areas designed to make hospital visits less unsettling.</p>\r\n"
  },
